The wave function ψ is a complex flux region over configuration space. It is not a physical wave — it is a probability amplitude field. Measurement is the projection operation: the flux region ψ collapses to a singulon (definite outcome) with probability |ψ|². The Heisenberg uncertainty principle is a null manifold: the product of position and momentum spread has a geometric lower bound.
Governing law — Born Probability Rule:  P(x) = |ψ(x,t)|²  ⇒  Projection(Φ_ψ, Σ_{position-basis})
Measurement is a projection operation in LangOne terms. The complex flux region ψ is projected onto the measurement basis, yielding a discrete singulon outcome. The probability is the squared amplitude — the "intensity" of the flux region at the projected point.

