The Schrödinger equation is the master gradient flow of M_QM. The Hamiltonian H is the energy operator — it generates the gradient flow of the wave function flux region through time. Stationary states are attractors: the spatial part φ_n(x) is fixed and only the phase rotates as a resonance lattice. Energy quantization arises from the boundary-condition null manifolds that restrict the allowed eigenvalues.
Governing law — Schrödinger Equation:  iℏ∂ψ/∂t = Hψ  ⇒  ∇F(H): master evolution operator of M_QM
The imaginary unit i encodes the 90° phase rotation between position and momentum representations — the symplectic structure of quantum phase space. This is the quantum analogue of Hamilton's equations in M_hamiltonian.

5.3  Quantum Operators and Observables
M_observables  Quantum Observables
Dominant primitives:  Σ(eigenvalue) + T(operator⋕state) + N(commutator-uncertainty)
Observable A: Hermitian operator on M_QM  (A† = A)
Eigenequation: A|a⟩ = a|a⟩  (eigenvalue singulon a from eigenstate |a⟩)
<A> = ⟨ψ|A|ψ⟩ = ∫ψ* Aψ dx  (expectation = projection of flux region)
