Entanglement is a non-separable tensor bond between two quantum flux regions. The null manifold on separability means the joint state cannot be decomposed into individual states. Bell's theorem shows that quantum tensor bonds exceed classical correlations: the joint field geometry is not reproducible by any local hidden variable model.
Governing law — Bell's Theorem:  QM: |CHSH| ≤ 2√2 > 2 (classical N)  ⇒  T_{AB} is genuinely non-local
Bell's theorem is a geometric statement: the non-local tensor bond structure of entanglement cannot be replicated by any classical (separable) field configuration. This is the deepest empirical confirmation that the LangOne tensor bond primitive describes a real feature of M_phys.

